Practise essential Linux command-line operations \endash  file and directory management, viewing files, searching, archiving, downloading, permissions, and environment variables.\par
\par
\fs28\b\cf2 Environment Used\b0\cf1\fs24\par
All commands below were run in a Linux terminal (WSL / Ubuntu). The exact same commands are saved as ready-to-run shell scripts in the \f1 scripts/\f0  folder of this project (see \f1 README.md\f0  for the full list), so the project can be re-run and re-verified at any time.\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 1: Creating and Renaming Files/Directories\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  \f1 mkdir\f0  creates a new, empty directory. \f1 touch\f0  creates a new, empty file (or updates its timestamp if it already exists). \f1 mv\f0  moves a file/directory to a new location \endash  when the source and destination are in the same folder, \f1 mv\f0  effectively renames the file.\par
\par
\b Commands used:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 mkdir test_dir\line touch test_dir/example.txt\line mv test_dir/example.txt test_dir/renamed_example.txt\line ls -l test_dir\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b What happens:\b0  A folder named \f1 test_dir\f0  is created. Inside it, an empty file \f1 example.txt\f0  is created, then immediately renamed to \f1 renamed_example.txt\f0 . The final \f1 ls -l\f0  confirms only \f1 renamed_example.txt\f0  exists.\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 1: paste a screenshot here showing the mkdir / touch / mv / ls -l commands and their output. Source: scripts/01_create_rename.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 2: Viewing File Contents\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  \f1 cat\f0  prints the entire contents of a file to the screen. \f1 head -n 5\f0  prints only the first 5 lines. \f1 tail -n 5\f0  prints only the last 5 lines. These are useful for quickly inspecting large files without opening a full text editor.\par
\par
\b Command \endash  full file:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 cat /etc/passwd\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 2a: cat /etc/passwd output]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\b Command \endash  first 5 lines:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 head -n 5 /etc/passwd\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 2b: head -n 5 /etc/passwd output]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\b Command \endash  last 5 lines:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 tail -n 5 /etc/passwd\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 2c: tail -n 5 /etc/passwd output. Source: scripts/02_view_files.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 3: Searching for Patterns\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  \f1 grep\f0  searches the given file line-by-line for a pattern (here, the plain text "root") and prints every matching line. It is one of the most-used tools for finding information inside text/log files.\par
\par
\b Command used:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 grep "root" /etc/passwd\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b What happens:\b0  Every line in \f1 /etc/passwd\f0  that contains the word "root" is printed \endash  typically the root user's own entry, plus any other accounts/comments that happen to include that word.\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 3: grep "root" /etc/passwd output. Source: scripts/03_search_grep.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 4: Zipping and Unzipping\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  \f1 zip -r\f0  recursively compresses a directory (and everything inside it) into a single \f1 .zip\f0  archive. \f1 unzip\f0  extracts the contents of a \f1 .zip\f0  archive into a target directory using the \f1 -d\f0  flag.\par
\par
\b Command \endash  compress:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 zip -r test_dir.zip test_dir\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 4a: zip -r test_dir.zip test_dir output]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\b Command \endash  extract:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 unzip test_dir.zip -d unzipped_dir\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b What happens:\b0  \f1 test_dir.zip\f0  is created containing a compressed copy of \f1 test_dir/\f0 . It is then extracted into a brand-new folder called \f1 unzipped_dir/\f0 , recreating the original folder structure inside it.\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 4b: unzip test_dir.zip -d unzipped_dir output, plus a directory listing proving unzipped_dir/test_dir/renamed_example.txt exists. Source: scripts/04_zip_unzip.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 5: Downloading Files\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  \f1 wget\f0  downloads a file from a URL over HTTP/HTTPS/FTP directly from the command line, without needing a browser. The \f1 -O\f0  flag lets you choose the saved filename.\par
\par
\b Command used:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 wget https://example.com/sample.txt -O sample.txt\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b Note:\b0  \f1 example.com\f0  is a placeholder/demo domain and does not actually host a file at \f1 /sample.txt\f0 , so \f1 wget\f0  may report a 404 error \endash  this still correctly demonstrates the command's usage and syntax. A working alternative used for a guaranteed successful download is noted as a commented-out fallback URL inside \f1 scripts/05_download.sh\f0 .\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 5: wget command and its output (connection log + resulting file). Source: scripts/05_download.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
