\fs32\b\cf2 Task 6: Changing Permissions\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  Every Linux file has three permission groups \endash  owner, group, and others \endash  each with read (r), write (w), and execute (x) permissions. \f1 chmod\f0  changes those permissions. The numeric mode \f1 444\f0  means read-only (r--r--r--) for owner, group, and others alike \endash  i.e. read-only for everyone, with no write or execute permission for anyone.\par
\par
\b Commands used:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 touch secure.txt\line ls -l secure.txt\line chmod 444 secure.txt\line ls -l secure.txt\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b What happens:\b0  Before \f1 chmod\f0 , a new file typically has \f1 rw-r--r--\f0  (644) permissions. After running \f1 chmod 444\f0 , the \f1 ls -l\f0  output changes to \f1 r--r--r--\f0 , confirming write access has been removed for everyone.\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 6: ls -l secure.txt BEFORE and AFTER chmod 444, showing the permission bits change. Source: scripts/06_permissions.sh]\b0\cf1\par
\par
\page
\fs32\b\cf2 Task 7: Working with Environment Variables\b0\cf1\fs24\par
\par
\b Explanation:\b0  Environment variables store values (like configuration settings or shell-wide data) that programs and shell sessions can read. \f1 export\f0  both creates/updates a variable and makes it available to the current shell and any child processes launched from it. \f1 echo $VARNAME\f0  prints its value.\par
\par
\b Commands used:\b0\par
{\f1\fs20\cf4 export MY_VAR="Hello, Linux!"\line echo $MY_VAR\par
}\cf1\fs24\par
\b Note:\b0  For an authentic screenshot, these two lines were also typed directly into the interactive terminal (in addition to running via \f1 scripts/07_env_vars.sh\f0 ), since \f1 export\f0  only persists for the current shell/its children \endash  running it purely inside a script would not keep the variable set in the parent terminal afterwards.\par
\par
\b\cf3 [SCREENSHOT PLACEHOLDER \endash  Task 7: export MY_VAR="Hello, Linux!" followed by echo $MY_VAR printing "Hello, Linux!". Source: scripts/07_env_vars.sh]\b0\cf1\par
